  var BU5_QUESTION_BANK = [
    { q: 'The main role of operations management is best described as:', options: ['Planning and controlling the processes that transform inputs into the goods and services a business sells', 'Recruiting and training all new employees', 'Setting the price of the firm’s products', 'Raising long-term finance through share issues'], correct: 0, explain: 'Operations management is responsible for the transformation process — converting inputs (materials, labour, capital) into outputs (goods and services) as efficiently as possible, while meeting quality, cost and delivery targets.', misconception: 'Operations management is sometimes confused with human resources or finance functions, when its core focus is the production/transformation process itself.', tag: 'Role of operations' },
    { q: 'A furniture workshop that builds one unique, hand-crafted kitchen at a time to a customer’s exact specification is using:', options: ['Job production', 'Batch production', 'Mass/flow production', 'Mass customization'], correct: 0, explain: 'Job production involves making a single, unique product to order, often highly customized and requiring skilled labour; it gives high quality and flexibility but is usually slow and expensive per unit.', misconception: 'Students sometimes label any small-scale or customized output as "mass customization", when true job production means one unique item is made from start to finish before the next begins.', tag: 'Job production' },
    { q: 'A bakery makes 200 chocolate cakes on Monday, then switches its ovens and staff to make 300 fruit cakes on Tuesday. This is an example of:', options: ['Batch production', 'Job production', 'Mass/flow production', 'Lean production'], correct: 0, explain: 'Batch production makes a group (batch) of identical items before switching the process to produce a different batch, offering more variety than flow production while gaining some economies of scale over job production.', misconception: 'It is easy to confuse batch production with flow production simply because quantities are larger than a single job — the key feature of batch production is that the process is reset between different product batches.', tag: 'Batch production' },
    { q: 'Which production method is most associated with a continuously moving assembly line producing large volumes of a standardized, identical product?', options: ['Mass/flow production', 'Job production', 'Batch production', 'Craft production'], correct: 0, explain: 'Mass/flow production uses a continuous, standardized process (often an assembly line) to produce very high volumes of identical output, achieving low unit costs through economies of scale but with little flexibility.', misconception: 'Some assume any large business must use flow production, but a large firm can still use batch or job methods if its products are varied or customized.', tag: 'Mass/flow production' },
    { q: 'A car manufacturer lets customers choose their own combination of paint colour, wheels and interior trim, which are then assembled on a largely standardized production line. This is an example of:', options: ['Mass customization', 'Job production', 'Batch production', 'Outsourcing'], correct: 0, explain: 'Mass customization combines the low unit costs of mass production with an element of customer choice, using flexible, often technology-enabled production lines to build variations of a largely standardized product.', misconception: 'Mass customization is sometimes confused with job production; unlike job production, mass customization still relies on a largely standardized, high-volume process rather than making each item from scratch.', tag: 'Mass customization' },
    { q: 'In lean production, "kaizen" refers to:', options: ['A process of continuous, incremental improvement involving all employees', 'A one-off major restructuring of the factory layout', 'A method of quality inspection carried out only by managers', 'A system for outsourcing production to another country'], correct: 0, explain: 'Kaizen is the Japanese philosophy of continuous improvement, where small, ongoing changes suggested by employees at all levels gradually reduce waste and improve efficiency.', misconception: 'Kaizen is sometimes mistaken for a single large-scale reorganization, when its defining feature is small, continuous, employee-driven improvements rather than one big change.', tag: 'Kaizen (HL)' },
    { q: 'Just-in-time (JIT) production aims to:', options: ['Minimize stock holding by receiving materials only as they are needed in the production process', 'Hold large buffer stocks to guarantee production never stops', 'Produce goods in large batches to maximize economies of scale', 'Outsource all manufacturing to reduce fixed costs'], correct: 0, explain: 'JIT is a lean technique in which materials and components arrive exactly when needed for production, cutting stockholding costs and waste, though it relies on very reliable suppliers and can leave a business vulnerable to supply disruptions.', misconception: 'JIT is sometimes wrongly associated with holding a safety buffer of stock; in fact its whole purpose is to minimize, not guarantee, stock levels.', tag: 'Just-in-time (HL)' },
    { q: '"Cradle to cradle" design and manufacturing refers to:', options: ['Designing products so that, at the end of their life, all materials can be reused or recycled into new products', 'Designing products to break down shortly after the warranty expires', 'A stock control system based on reorder levels', 'A method of quality inspection at the end of the production line'], correct: 0, explain: 'Cradle to cradle design aims to eliminate waste by designing products and processes so materials can be continually recycled or reused, reducing environmental impact across the whole product lifecycle rather than just at the point of sale.', misconception: 'Cradle to cradle is sometimes confused with simple recycling of waste materials, but it specifically means designing the product from the start so its materials can re-enter a new production cycle.', tag: 'Cradle to cradle (HL)' },
    { q: 'The key difference between quality control and quality assurance is that:', options: ['Quality control inspects output after production, while quality assurance builds quality checks into the process throughout production', 'Quality control is only used in service industries, while quality assurance is only used in manufacturing', 'Quality assurance is carried out solely by external auditors', 'Quality control focuses on marketing, while quality assurance focuses on finance'], correct: 0, explain: 'Quality control traditionally involves inspecting finished goods for defects at the end of the process, whereas quality assurance is a more proactive approach embedding quality checks and standards throughout the entire production process to prevent defects occurring.', misconception: 'Quality control and quality assurance are often used interchangeably, but IB Business Management distinguishes them by when and how quality is checked — after the fact versus built in throughout.', tag: 'Quality control vs assurance (HL)' },
    { q: 'A "quality circle" is best described as:', options: ['A small group of employees who meet regularly to identify and solve quality-related problems', 'A department solely responsible for final product inspection', 'An external body that awards national quality certificates', 'A single manager appointed to oversee all quality issues'], correct: 0, explain: 'Quality circles are small, voluntary groups of workers (often from the same area) who meet to discuss and suggest solutions to quality and production problems, drawing on their first-hand experience of the process.', misconception: 'Quality circles are sometimes mistaken for a management-only inspection team, but their defining feature is that they involve front-line employees working together to solve problems.', tag: 'Quality circles (HL)' },
    { q: 'Benchmarking as a method of managing quality involves:', options: ['Comparing a firm’s processes and performance against the best practice of other organizations to identify areas for improvement', 'Setting arbitrary internal targets with no external comparison', 'Inspecting every single unit of output before it leaves the factory', 'Reducing stock levels to zero at all times'], correct: 0, explain: 'Benchmarking compares a business’s products, processes or performance against those of leading competitors or best-practice organizations, helping identify gaps and set improvement targets.', misconception: 'Benchmarking is sometimes confused with simple goal-setting; the key feature is that the standard being aimed for is drawn from an external best-practice comparison, not an internally invented target.', tag: 'Benchmarking (HL)' },
    { q: 'Total quality management (TQM) is best defined as:', options: ['An organization-wide culture and set of practices aiming for continuous improvement and zero defects, with quality as the responsibility of every employee', 'A final inspection stage carried out only by the quality control department', 'A one-off certification process completed when a factory opens', 'A pricing strategy based on offering the lowest price in the market'], correct: 0, explain: 'TQM is a holistic management philosophy in which every employee, at every stage of production, takes responsibility for quality, with the goal of continuous improvement and eliminating defects rather than just inspecting for them afterwards.', misconception: 'TQM is sometimes reduced to "the quality department’s job", but its defining principle is that quality is everyone’s responsibility across the whole organization.', tag: 'TQM (HL)' },
    { q: 'A likely benefit of successfully implementing lean production and TQM in a manufacturing business is:', options: ['Lower unit costs and fewer defects due to reduced waste and continuous improvement', 'Guaranteed higher selling prices regardless of competition', 'The complete elimination of the need for skilled staff', 'Automatic exemption from health and safety regulations'], correct: 0, explain: 'By cutting waste (lean) and embedding a culture of continuous, organization-wide quality improvement (TQM), a business can lower unit costs, reduce defect rates and improve customer satisfaction, though implementation can be costly and requires staff buy-in.', misconception: 'Some assume lean/TQM benefits are automatic and costless, when in reality they require investment in training and can fail without genuine employee commitment.', tag: 'Lean and TQM impact (HL)' },
    { q: 'National and international quality standards (such as ISO certification) are important to a business mainly because:', options: ['They provide independent recognition of consistent quality, which can build trust with customers and open up new markets', 'They guarantee the business will make a profit', 'They remove the need for any further quality checks', 'They are only relevant to government-owned organizations'], correct: 0, explain: 'Meeting recognized quality standards signals to customers, suppliers and international partners that a business meets consistent, independently verified quality benchmarks, which can be a condition of trading with larger firms or entering export markets.', misconception: 'Gaining a quality certification is sometimes seen as the end of quality management, when in practice standards require ongoing compliance and do not guarantee profitability.', tag: 'Quality standards (HL)' },
    { q: 'Which of the following is most likely to influence a firm’s choice of production location?', options: ['The cost and availability of labour, land and raw materials near the site', 'The personal star sign of the founder', 'The number of social media followers the brand has', 'The colour scheme used in the firm’s logo'], correct: 0, explain: 'Location decisions are typically driven by factors such as the cost and availability of labour, proximity to raw materials and markets, infrastructure, government incentives and legal/political conditions.', misconception: 'Students can overlook that location decisions are usually driven by a combination of cost and market-access factors, not a single consideration in isolation.', tag: 'Location decisions' },
    { q: 'A firm that hires an external, independent company to manufacture its products rather than making them itself is practising:', options: ['Outsourcing/subcontracting', 'Insourcing', 'Reshoring', 'Offshoring'], correct: 0, explain: 'Outsourcing (subcontracting) means paying an external, separate organization to carry out an activity — such as manufacturing — that could otherwise be done in-house, which can reduce costs but may reduce control over quality.', misconception: 'Outsourcing is sometimes confused with offshoring; outsourcing is about using an external company, whereas offshoring specifically means relocating production to another country (which can be done in-house or outsourced).', tag: 'Outsourcing' },
    { q: 'A US clothing brand closes its domestic factory and relocates production to its own newly built factory in Bangladesh to cut labour costs. This is an example of:', options: ['Offshoring', 'Outsourcing', 'Insourcing', 'Reshoring'], correct: 0, explain: 'Offshoring is the relocation of a business activity, such as production, to another country, often to take advantage of lower costs, while the activity may still be carried out in-house by the same company.', misconception: 'Offshoring is sometimes assumed to always involve using another firm, but it can be done entirely in-house — the defining feature is the move to another country, not the use of an external supplier.', tag: 'Offshoring' },
    { q: 'A company that previously outsourced its customer service to an external call centre decides to bring that function back under its own direct control and staffing is practising:', options: ['Insourcing', 'Offshoring', 'Reshoring', 'Benchmarking'], correct: 0, explain: 'Insourcing is the reverse of outsourcing: a business takes back control of an activity previously carried out by an external contractor, often to improve quality control or reduce dependency on a third party.', misconception: 'Insourcing is sometimes confused with reshoring; insourcing is about who performs the activity (in-house versus external), while reshoring is specifically about relocating production back to the home country.', tag: 'Insourcing' },
    { q: 'A manufacturer that moved production overseas ten years ago now decides to relocate that production back to its home country, partly due to rising overseas wages and shipping costs. This is known as:', options: ['Reshoring', 'Offshoring', 'Outsourcing', 'Insourcing'], correct: 0, explain: 'Reshoring is the process of bringing production that had previously been offshored back to the firm’s home country, often triggered by rising overseas costs, supply chain risk, or a wish for closer control over quality and delivery.', misconception: 'Reshoring is sometimes confused with insourcing; reshoring specifically concerns the geographic location of production moving back home, regardless of whether it is done in-house or subcontracted.', tag: 'Reshoring' },
    { q: 'Total contribution is calculated as:', options: ['Contribution per unit multiplied by the number of units sold', 'Selling price multiplied by the number of units sold', 'Fixed costs divided by contribution per unit', 'Total revenue minus total costs'], correct: 0, explain: 'Contribution per unit (selling price minus variable cost per unit) shows how much each unit contributes towards covering fixed costs and generating profit; total contribution scales this up across all units sold.', misconception: 'Total contribution is sometimes confused with total revenue, but contribution only reflects the amount left after variable costs, before fixed costs are covered.', tag: 'Contribution' },
    { q: 'On a standard break-even chart, the break-even point is found where:', options: ['The total revenue line crosses the total cost line', 'The total revenue line crosses the fixed cost line', 'The variable cost line crosses the horizontal axis', 'The fixed cost line crosses the total revenue line at the origin'], correct: 0, explain: 'The break-even point on a break-even chart is the level of output at which total revenue exactly equals total costs (fixed plus variable), meaning the business makes neither a profit nor a loss.', misconception: 'Some students look for where revenue crosses the fixed cost line rather than the total cost line, forgetting that total costs include both fixed and variable costs.', tag: 'Break-even chart' },
    { q: 'A business has fixed costs of $12,000, sells its product for $50, and has a variable cost per unit of $30. What is its break-even quantity?', options: ['400 units', '500 units', '600 units', '240 units'], correct: 2, explain: 'Contribution per unit = selling price − variable cost = $50 − $30 = $20. Break-even quantity = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit = $12,000 ÷ $20 = 600 units.', misconception: 'A common error is dividing fixed costs by the variable cost per unit ($12,000 ÷ $30 ≈ 400) or by the selling price ($12,000 ÷ $50 = 240) instead of by the contribution per unit.', tag: 'Break-even quantity' },
    { q: 'A firm has fixed costs of $12,000, sells its product for $50 with a variable cost per unit of $30, and sells 1,000 units. What is its profit or loss?', options: ['$8,000 profit', '$20,000 profit', '$12,000 profit', '$8,000 loss'], correct: 0, explain: 'Contribution per unit = $50 − $30 = $20. Total contribution = 1,000 × $20 = $20,000. Profit = total contribution − fixed costs = $20,000 − $12,000 = $8,000 profit.', misconception: 'It is easy to stop after calculating total contribution ($20,000) and forget to subtract fixed costs to find the actual profit.', tag: 'Break-even profit/loss' },
    { q: 'A business has a break-even quantity of 600 units and currently sells 1,000 units. What is its margin of safety?', options: ['1,600 units', '600 units', '400 units', '300 units'], correct: 2, explain: 'Margin of safety = actual (or budgeted) output − break-even quantity = 1,000 − 600 = 400 units. This shows how far sales can fall before the business makes a loss.', misconception: 'Margin of safety is sometimes calculated by adding rather than subtracting, or by using the break-even quantity alone, rather than comparing it to actual output.', tag: 'Margin of safety' },
    { q: 'A firm has fixed costs of $12,000, a contribution per unit of $20, and wants to earn a target profit of $10,000. What output must it sell to achieve this?', options: ['1,100 units', '500 units', '600 units', '440 units'], correct: 0, explain: 'Target profit output = (fixed costs + target profit) ÷ contribution per unit = ($12,000 + $10,000) ÷ $20 = $22,000 ÷ $20 = 1,100 units.', misconception: 'A common mistake is dividing only the target profit by the contribution per unit ($10,000 ÷ $20 = 500), forgetting that fixed costs must be covered first.', tag: 'Target profit output' },
    { q: 'A firm has fixed costs of $8,000, sells its product for $25 with a variable cost per unit of $15, and sells 1,500 units. What is its target profit at this output?', options: ['$7,000', '$15,000', '$8,000', '$23,000'], correct: 0, explain: 'Contribution per unit = $25 − $15 = $10. Total contribution = 1,500 × $10 = $15,000. Profit = total contribution − fixed costs = $15,000 − $8,000 = $7,000.', misconception: 'A common error is reporting the total contribution ($15,000) as the profit, forgetting to subtract fixed costs.', tag: 'Target profit' },
    { q: 'A firm wants to make a target profit of $5,000 on 500 units, with fixed costs of $10,000 and a variable cost per unit of $20. What target price must it charge?', options: ['$50', '$30', '$20', '$40'], correct: 0, explain: 'Total revenue needed = fixed costs + target profit + total variable costs = $10,000 + $5,000 + (500 × $20) = $25,000. Target price = $25,000 ÷ 500 units = $50, which equals variable cost per unit ($20) plus (fixed costs + target profit) ÷ output ($15,000 ÷ 500 = $30).', misconception: 'A frequent mistake is calculating only the fixed-cost-and-profit portion of the price ($30) and forgetting to add back the variable cost per unit, giving an incomplete target price.', tag: 'Target price' },
    { q: 'A firm’s fixed costs are $20,000 and variable cost per unit is $40. If the selling price rises from $60 to $80, what happens to the break-even quantity?', options: ['It falls from 1,000 to 500 units', 'It rises from 1,000 to 500 units', 'It stays at 1,000 units', 'It falls from 500 to 1,000 units'], correct: 0, explain: 'At $60: contribution = $20, break-even = $20,000 ÷ $20 = 1,000 units. At $80: contribution = $40, break-even = $20,000 ÷ $40 = 500 units. A higher price raises contribution per unit, so fewer units are needed to break even.', misconception: 'Students sometimes assume a higher price must raise the break-even quantity, when a higher contribution per unit actually lowers the output needed to cover fixed costs.', tag: 'Effects on break-even' },
    { q: 'A firm sells 2,000 units at $50 each, with fixed costs of $15,000. If variable cost per unit rises from $30 to $35, by how much does profit fall?', options: ['$10,000', '$5,000', '$15,000', '$25,000'], correct: 0, explain: 'Original profit: contribution $20 × 2,000 − $15,000 = $40,000 − $15,000 = $25,000. New profit: contribution $15 × 2,000 − $15,000 = $30,000 − $15,000 = $15,000. Profit falls by $25,000 − $15,000 = $10,000.', misconception: 'A common error is calculating only the change in contribution per unit ($5) without multiplying it by the full quantity sold (2,000 units) to find the total change in profit.', tag: 'Effects on profit' },
    { q: 'Which of the following is a genuine limitation of break-even analysis as a decision-making tool?', options: ['It assumes all output produced is sold and that costs and revenues are perfectly linear, which is rarely true in reality', 'It cannot be calculated without expensive specialist software', 'It only works for service businesses, not manufacturers', 'It requires the business to already be making a profit'], correct: 0, explain: 'Break-even analysis relies on simplifying assumptions — constant selling price, constant variable cost per unit, and that all output is sold — which rarely hold exactly in real markets, and it ignores external factors like competitor actions.', misconception: 'Break-even analysis is sometimes treated as a fully accurate forecasting tool, when its static, linear assumptions mean it should be used alongside other forms of analysis.', tag: 'Break-even limitations' },
    { q: 'The supply chain of a business refers to:', options: ['The sequence of processes and organizations involved in producing and delivering a good or service from raw material to final customer', 'The internal hierarchy of management within a single firm', 'The list of a firm’s shareholders', 'The set of laws governing consumer protection'], correct: 0, explain: 'A supply chain covers every stage — sourcing raw materials, manufacturing, distribution and delivery — across potentially many organizations, both locally and globally, before a product reaches the end consumer.', misconception: 'The supply chain is sometimes confused with a firm’s own internal production process, when it actually spans multiple organizations, from suppliers through to the final customer.', tag: 'Supply chain (HL)' },
    { q: 'Compared with just-in-time (JIT), a business using just-in-case (JIC) stock control:', options: ['Holds larger buffer stocks to guard against unexpected demand or supply disruption', 'Orders stock only at the exact moment it is needed on the production line', 'Has no stockholding costs at all', 'Relies entirely on suppliers being 100% reliable with zero lead time'], correct: 0, explain: 'JIC production deliberately holds buffer stocks of raw materials and finished goods as a safety margin against uncertain demand or unreliable supply, trading higher stockholding costs for greater security, unlike JIT which minimizes stock.', misconception: 'JIC is sometimes assumed to be the "old-fashioned, inefficient" option with no benefits, but it offers real protection against disruption that lean JIT systems are more vulnerable to.', tag: 'JIT vs JIC (HL)' },
    { q: 'On a stock control chart, "lead time" refers to:', options: ['The time delay between placing an order for stock and that stock actually arriving', 'The minimum stock level a business always keeps in reserve', 'The stock level at which a new order is triggered', 'The quantity of stock ordered each time an order is placed'], correct: 0, explain: 'Lead time is the gap between when an order is placed with a supplier and when the new stock is delivered and available for use; longer lead times generally require higher buffer stock and earlier reorder points.', misconception: 'Lead time is sometimes confused with buffer stock; lead time is a measure of time delay, not a quantity of stock held.', tag: 'Stock control: lead time (HL)' },
    { q: 'On a stock control chart, "buffer stock" refers to:', options: ['The minimum level of stock kept in reserve to cover unexpected demand or delivery delays', 'The exact quantity of stock ordered each time an order is placed', 'The stock level that automatically triggers a new order', 'The maximum stock level a warehouse is legally permitted to hold'], correct: 0, explain: 'Buffer stock is a safety margin of minimum stock held to protect against sudden increases in demand or delays in supplier delivery, reducing the risk of running out of stock (stockouts).', misconception: 'Buffer stock is sometimes confused with reorder level; buffer stock is the safety minimum a business never wants to fall below, while reorder level is the point at which a new order is placed.', tag: 'Stock control: buffer stock (HL)' },
    { q: 'On a stock control chart, the "reorder level" is:', options: ['The stock level at which a new order must be placed to avoid stock falling below the buffer level', 'The total amount of stock ordered in a single order', 'The average stock held across the year', 'The delay between ordering and receiving stock'], correct: 0, explain: 'The reorder level is set high enough above the buffer stock that, given the expected lead time, a new order placed at that point will arrive before stock falls below the safety (buffer) level.', misconception: 'Students sometimes confuse reorder level with reorder quantity — reorder level is the trigger point (a stock level), while reorder quantity is the amount ordered each time.', tag: 'Stock control: reorder level (HL)' },
    { q: 'On a stock control chart, the "reorder quantity" is:', options: ['The fixed amount of stock ordered from a supplier each time an order is placed', 'The stock level that triggers a new order', 'The time taken for an order to be delivered', 'The absolute minimum stock a firm can hold before running out'], correct: 0, explain: 'Reorder quantity is the specific amount of stock a business orders each time it places an order, often set to balance ordering costs against stockholding costs.', misconception: 'Reorder quantity is sometimes confused with reorder level; reorder quantity is "how much" is ordered, while reorder level is "when" (at what stock level) an order is triggered.', tag: 'Stock control: reorder quantity (HL)' },
    { q: 'A factory has a maximum possible output of 10,000 units per month but actually produces 7,500 units this month. What is its capacity utilization rate?', options: ['75%', '133%', '25%', '70%'], correct: 0, explain: 'Capacity utilization rate = (actual output ÷ maximum possible output) × 100 = (7,500 ÷ 10,000) × 100 = 75%.', misconception: 'A common mistake is inverting the fraction (maximum ÷ actual), which gives 133% instead of the correct 75%.', tag: 'Capacity utilization (HL)' },
    { q: 'Out of 3,000 units produced in a batch, 45 are found to be defective. What is the defect rate?', options: ['1.5%', '15%', '0.15%', '4.5%'], correct: 0, explain: 'Defect rate = (number of defective units ÷ total units produced) × 100 = (45 ÷ 3,000) × 100 = 1.5%.', misconception: 'A misplaced decimal point is a common error here, giving 15% or 0.15% instead of the correct 1.5%.', tag: 'Defect rate (HL)' },
    { q: 'A factory produces 12,000 units in a month using 60 employees. What is its labour productivity?', options: ['200 units per employee', '60 units per employee', '12,000 units per employee', '20 units per employee'], correct: 0, explain: 'Labour productivity = total output ÷ number of employees = 12,000 ÷ 60 = 200 units per employee.', misconception: 'Labour productivity is sometimes reported as simply the number of employees or the raw output figure, rather than output divided by the number of employees.', tag: 'Labour productivity (HL)' },
    { q: 'A factory produces 8,000 units using 4 machines. What is its capital productivity?', options: ['2,000 units per machine', '4 units per machine', '8,000 units per machine', '32,000 units per machine'], correct: 0, explain: 'Capital productivity = total output ÷ units of capital employed = 8,000 ÷ 4 machines = 2,000 units per machine.', misconception: 'A common error is multiplying output by the number of machines (8,000 × 4 = 32,000) instead of dividing.', tag: 'Capital productivity (HL)' },
    { q: 'A team is expected to produce 6,000 units in a week (its standard/expected output) but actually produces 4,800 units. What is its productivity rate?', options: ['80%', '125%', '48%', '60%'], correct: 0, explain: 'Productivity rate = (actual output ÷ expected/standard output) × 100 = (4,800 ÷ 6,000) × 100 = 80%.', misconception: 'Inverting the fraction (expected ÷ actual) gives 125% instead of the correct 80%, wrongly suggesting output exceeded the target.', tag: 'Productivity rate (HL)' },
    { q: 'A firm generates total contribution of $60,000 and net operating profit of $20,000. What is its degree of operating leverage?', options: ['3', '0.33', '20,000', '80,000'], correct: 0, explain: 'Operating leverage = total contribution ÷ net operating profit = $60,000 ÷ $20,000 = 3, meaning profit is highly sensitive to changes in sales.', misconception: 'A common error is inverting the ratio (net profit ÷ contribution = 0.33) or simply quoting one of the raw figures instead of calculating the ratio between them.', tag: 'Operating leverage (HL)' },
    { q: 'A firm needs 5,000 units. Producing them in-house would cost $8,000 in fixed costs plus $4 variable cost per unit; buying them from a supplier would cost $6 per unit. Based on cost alone, what should the firm do?', options: ['Make in-house, since CTM ($28,000) is lower than CTB ($30,000)', 'Buy from the supplier, since CTB ($30,000) is lower than CTM ($28,000)', 'Make in-house, since CTM ($36,000) is lower than CTB ($30,000)', 'Buy from the supplier, since CTB ($20,000) is lower than CTM ($28,000)'], correct: 0, explain: 'Cost to make (CTM) = fixed costs + (variable cost per unit × quantity) = $8,000 + ($4 × 5,000) = $28,000. Cost to buy (CTB) = price per unit × quantity = $6 × 5,000 = $30,000. Since CTM is lower, the firm should make in-house.', misconception: 'A common mistake is forgetting to include the fixed costs of in-house production when calculating CTM, which can make buying look cheaper than it really is.', tag: 'Cost to buy vs make (HL)' },
    { q: 'The key difference between crisis management and contingency planning is that:', options: ['Contingency planning prepares in advance for potential crises, while crisis management deals with an actual crisis as it unfolds', 'Crisis management only applies to natural disasters', 'Contingency planning happens only after a crisis has occurred', 'There is no meaningful difference between the two terms'], correct: 0, explain: 'Contingency planning is proactive, involving preparation of plans and procedures before a crisis occurs, whereas crisis management is reactive, involving the actual decisions and actions taken to deal with a crisis once it has happened.', misconception: 'The two terms are sometimes used interchangeably, but the syllabus distinguishes them clearly by timing: before the event (contingency planning) versus during/after it (crisis management).', tag: 'Crisis vs contingency (HL)' },
    { q: 'During a crisis, "transparency" is important because:', options: ['Being open and honest with stakeholders about what has happened helps maintain trust and reduces damaging speculation', 'It allows a business to legally avoid disclosing any information', 'It guarantees that the crisis will be resolved within 24 hours', 'It removes the need for any further communication with stakeholders'], correct: 0, explain: 'Transparency during a crisis means openly sharing accurate, timely information with stakeholders; this helps preserve trust and reputation, whereas concealment or delay often makes a crisis worse when the truth eventually emerges.', misconception: 'Some assume transparency means the crisis is automatically resolved faster, when its real benefit is protecting stakeholder trust and reputation, not speeding up the technical fix.', tag: 'Crisis: transparency (HL)' },
    { q: 'Effective communication during a crisis primarily helps a business by:', options: ['Ensuring accurate, consistent information reaches employees, customers and the media, reducing confusion and rumour', 'Allowing the firm to avoid speaking to the media entirely', 'Guaranteeing that no stakeholder will be unhappy with the outcome', 'Replacing the need for any actual corrective action'], correct: 0, explain: 'Clear, consistent, well-timed communication to all relevant stakeholders reduces uncertainty and the spread of misinformation, and helps coordinate the response internally and externally.', misconception: 'Communication is sometimes mistaken for public relations "spin" designed to avoid blame, when effective crisis communication should be accurate and consistent, not evasive.', tag: 'Crisis: communication (HL)' },
    { q: 'Why is speed of response important in effective crisis management?', options: ['Acting quickly can limit the scale of damage and prevent the situation from escalating further', 'A fast response guarantees the crisis will have no financial cost', 'Speed matters only for crises caused by natural disasters', 'Responding quickly removes the need for a full investigation afterwards'], correct: 0, explain: 'A rapid initial response can contain a crisis before it spreads or worsens — for example, quickly recalling a faulty product — reducing harm to customers, stakeholders and the firm’s reputation.', misconception: 'Speed is sometimes assumed to eliminate all consequences, when a fast response reduces but does not remove the underlying costs and reputational impact of a crisis.', tag: 'Crisis: speed (HL)' },
    { q: '"Control" as a factor in effective crisis management refers to:', options: ['A designated individual or team taking clear charge of coordinating the response so actions are consistent and well-organized', 'Allowing every department to respond independently without any coordination', 'Handing full control of the business over to government regulators', 'Freezing all business activity until the crisis is fully resolved'], correct: 0, explain: 'Effective crisis management usually requires a clear chain of command, with a designated crisis team or leader coordinating the response so that actions and messages are consistent rather than fragmented.', misconception: 'Control is sometimes confused with rigid inaction; in a crisis, "control" means coordinated, decisive leadership, not simply stopping all activity.', tag: 'Crisis: control (HL)' },
    { q: 'A key benefit of having a well-developed contingency plan is that it can reduce the:', options: ['Cost and time needed to respond effectively if a crisis actually occurs', 'Need for the business to ever hold insurance', 'Likelihood that any risk will ever materialize', 'Requirement to communicate with stakeholders during a crisis'], correct: 0, explain: 'Contingency planning prepares a business in advance, meaning that if a crisis does occur, the cost and time needed to respond can be significantly reduced because roles, procedures and resources are already identified.', misconception: 'Contingency planning is sometimes thought to prevent crises from happening at all, when its real value is reducing the cost, time and confusion of the response if one does occur.', tag: 'Contingency planning: cost/time (HL)' },
    { q: 'Evaluating the impact of contingency planning in terms of risk and safety would most likely focus on:', options: ['Whether the plan reduces the danger to employees, customers and the public if a crisis occurs', 'Whether the plan increases the firm’s advertising budget', 'Whether the plan eliminates the need for any staff training', 'Whether the plan guarantees a rise in the firm’s share price'], correct: 0, explain: 'A key purpose of contingency planning is to reduce risk and improve safety outcomes for employees, customers and the wider public by having tested procedures ready (such as evacuation plans or product recall protocols) before a crisis strikes.', misconception: 'Contingency planning’s risk and safety benefits are sometimes overlooked in favour of only considering its financial cost, when protecting people is often the primary justification for such planning.', tag: 'Contingency planning: risk/safety (HL)' },
    { q: 'Research and development (R&D) is important to a business primarily because it can:', options: ['Lead to new or improved products and processes that help the firm stay competitive', 'Guarantee the firm will never make a loss', 'Remove the need for any marketing spending', 'Automatically reduce the firm’s tax liability'], correct: 0, explain: 'R&D allows a business to innovate — developing new products, improving existing ones, or finding more efficient processes — which can help build competitive advantage, though R&D spending is risky and does not guarantee commercial success.', misconception: 'R&D success is sometimes assumed to be guaranteed simply because money is spent on it, when in reality much R&D investment does not lead to a commercially successful outcome.', tag: 'Research and development (HL)' },
    { q: 'Developing products that address customers’ "unmet needs, of which customers may or may not be aware" refers to:', options: ['Identifying needs that customers have not yet articulated or even fully recognized themselves, and designing products to meet them', 'Only ever asking customers directly what new product they want built', 'Copying a competitor’s best-selling product exactly', 'Reducing the price of an existing product until demand increases'], correct: 0, explain: 'Some of the most successful innovations meet needs customers could not describe in a survey because they did not yet know such a solution was possible; businesses use market research, observation and experimentation to uncover these latent needs.', misconception: 'Innovation is sometimes assumed to only come from directly asking customers what they want, when many breakthrough products address needs customers were not consciously aware they had.', tag: 'Unmet customer needs (HL)' },
    { q: 'Copyright, as a form of intellectual property protection, primarily protects:', options: ['Original creative works such as writing, music, art and software code from being copied without permission', 'A specific invention or new process from being copied by competitors', 'A brand name, logo or slogan from being used by another business', 'A company’s physical factory and equipment from being seized by creditors'], correct: 0, explain: 'Copyright automatically protects original creative and artistic works (such as books, music, films and software) from being reproduced or distributed without the creator’s permission, for a set period of time.', misconception: 'Copyright is sometimes confused with patents; copyright protects creative expression, whereas a patent protects a specific invention or functional process.', tag: 'Copyright (HL)' },
    { q: 'A patent, as a form of intellectual property protection, primarily grants:', options: ['The exclusive legal right to make, use or sell a specific invention for a set period of time', 'Permanent, unlimited ownership of a brand name', 'Automatic protection for any creative written or artistic work', 'The right to prevent competitors from ever entering the same industry'], correct: 0, explain: 'A patent gives an inventor the exclusive right to commercially exploit a specific invention or process for a limited period (often around 20 years), preventing others from making, using or selling it without permission.', misconception: 'Patents are sometimes assumed to last forever or to cover an entire industry, when they apply to a specific invention for a fixed, limited time period.', tag: 'Patents (HL)' },
    { q: 'A trademark, as a form of intellectual property protection, protects:', options: ['A distinctive brand name, logo, symbol or slogan that identifies a business’s goods or services', 'A specific manufacturing process or invention', 'An original piece of written or musical work', 'A company’s confidential financial records'], correct: 0, explain: 'A trademark legally protects distinctive branding elements — such as a name, logo or slogan — preventing competitors from using identical or confusingly similar marks that could mislead consumers.', misconception: 'Trademarks are sometimes confused with patents or copyright, but they specifically protect brand identity rather than inventions or creative works.', tag: 'Trademarks (HL)' },
    { q: 'A smartphone manufacturer releasing a slightly upgraded camera and battery life in its annual new model is an example of:', options: ['Incremental innovation', 'Disruptive innovation', 'Reshoring', 'Insourcing'], correct: 0, explain: 'Incremental innovation involves small, gradual improvements to existing products or processes, refining what already exists rather than fundamentally changing the market.', misconception: 'Incremental innovation is sometimes underestimated as "not real innovation", but consistent small improvements can be a major source of sustained competitive advantage.', tag: 'Incremental innovation (HL)' },
    { q: 'A streaming service that fundamentally changes how consumers watch television, making traditional broadcast TV largely obsolete, is an example of:', options: ['Disruptive innovation', 'Incremental innovation', 'Outsourcing', 'Benchmarking'], correct: 0, explain: 'Disruptive innovation creates a new market or way of doing things that significantly displaces existing products, technologies or business models, often starting in a niche before overturning the whole industry.', misconception: 'Disruptive innovation is sometimes assumed to simply mean "a big improvement", but its defining feature is that it fundamentally changes or displaces the existing market rather than just improving on it.', tag: 'Disruptive innovation (HL)' },
    { q: 'In a management information systems context, "data analytics" refers to:', options: ['The process of examining data sets to draw conclusions and support business decision-making', 'The physical storage of paper business records', 'A legal requirement to publish annual financial statements', 'A method of manually counting stock in a warehouse'], correct: 0, explain: 'Data analytics involves systematically analysing raw data — often using software tools — to identify patterns, trends and insights that can inform business decisions across marketing, operations, HR and finance.', misconception: 'Data analytics is sometimes confused with simply collecting data; the key feature is the analysis and interpretation of that data to generate useful insight.', tag: 'Data analytics (HL)' },
    { q: 'A "database" is best defined as:', options: ['An organized collection of structured data stored electronically so it can be easily accessed, managed and updated', 'A single physical filing cabinet of paper records', 'A firm’s annual report to shareholders', 'A type of production line used in flow production'], correct: 0, explain: 'A database is a structured, typically digital collection of data organized so it can be efficiently stored, searched, updated and retrieved, forming the backbone of most modern management information systems.', misconception: 'A database is sometimes thought of as any pile of information, when its defining feature is that the data is structured and organized for efficient retrieval and management.', tag: 'Database (HL)' },
    { q: 'Cybersecurity refers to a business’s efforts to:', options: ['Protect its computer systems, networks and data from unauthorized access, damage or theft (cybercrime)', 'Increase the speed of its physical delivery vehicles', 'Reduce the number of employees needed in its IT department to zero', 'Guarantee that its website never needs to be updated'], correct: 0, explain: 'Cybersecurity encompasses the technologies, processes and practices designed to protect systems, networks, and data from cybercrime such as hacking, data breaches and malware, which can otherwise cause significant financial and reputational damage.', misconception: 'Cybersecurity is sometimes seen as purely an IT department issue, when data breaches can have serious consequences for the whole business, including legal liability and loss of customer trust.', tag: 'Cybersecurity (HL)' },
    { q: '"Virtual reality" in a business context refers to technology that:', options: ['Creates an immersive, computer-generated simulated environment that users can interact with', 'Connects everyday physical devices to the internet so they can share data', 'Analyses extremely large and complex data sets to find patterns', 'Automatically encrypts a company’s confidential files'], correct: 0, explain: 'Virtual reality uses headsets and software to immerse users in a fully simulated digital environment, which businesses can use for training, product design, or customer experiences such as virtual showrooms.', misconception: 'Virtual reality is sometimes confused with the internet of things; VR is about immersive simulated environments, while IoT is about connecting physical devices to share data.', tag: 'Virtual reality (HL)' },
    { q: 'The "internet of things" (IoT) refers to:', options: ['A network of everyday physical devices and objects connected to the internet, able to collect and exchange data', 'A single company’s internal email system', 'A method for protecting a business from cyberattacks', 'A type of stock control chart used in production planning'], correct: 0, explain: 'The internet of things describes the growing network of physical objects — from factory sensors to household appliances — embedded with technology that lets them connect to the internet and share data, enabling smarter monitoring and automation.', misconception: 'IoT is sometimes confused with general "the internet"; its distinguishing feature is physical devices and objects, not just websites or people, being connected and exchanging data.', tag: 'Internet of things (HL)' },
    { q: 'Artificial intelligence (AI), as used in business, refers to:', options: ['Computer systems designed to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as recognizing patterns or making decisions', 'Any computer that is connected to the internet', 'A stock control system that reorders materials automatically at a fixed calendar date', 'A method of protecting intellectual property'], correct: 0, explain: 'Artificial intelligence involves software systems capable of tasks that typically require human-like intelligence — such as learning from data, recognizing patterns, or making predictions — and is increasingly used in areas like customer service chatbots and demand forecasting.', misconception: 'AI is sometimes assumed to mean any automated or computerized system, when its defining feature is performing tasks that would normally require human-like reasoning or learning.', tag: 'Artificial intelligence (HL)' },
    { q: '"Big data" refers to:', options: ['Extremely large, complex data sets that require specialized tools and techniques to store, process and analyse', 'A single very large spreadsheet file', 'The total revenue earned by a multinational company', 'A government regulation limiting how much data a firm can collect'], correct: 0, explain: 'Big data describes data sets so large and complex in volume, variety and velocity that traditional data-processing tools cannot handle them effectively, requiring specialized analytics software to extract useful business insight.', misconception: 'Big data is sometimes thought to simply mean "a lot of data", but the concept specifically refers to data whose scale and complexity require specialized tools beyond standard spreadsheets or databases.', tag: 'Big data (HL)' },
    { q: '"Digital Taylorism" refers to the use of data and technology to:', options: ['Closely monitor and measure individual employees’ performance and productivity, often in real time', 'Automatically design new product packaging', 'Protect a business from cybercrime', 'Calculate a firm’s break-even point'], correct: 0, explain: 'Digital Taylorism applies principles similar to Frederick Taylor’s scientific management — but using modern data collection technology — to closely track, measure and manage employees’ work rate and performance, which can raise productivity but also raises concerns about employee privacy and wellbeing.', misconception: 'Digital Taylorism is sometimes assumed to be purely positive for efficiency, when it also carries significant ethical concerns around employee surveillance, stress and trust.', tag: 'Digital Taylorism (HL)' },
    { q: 'Using "data mining" to inform business decision-making involves:', options: ['Searching through large data sets to discover hidden patterns and relationships that can guide strategic and operational decisions', 'Physically extracting raw materials such as metals or minerals', 'Deleting outdated customer records to save storage space', 'Manually surveying a small sample of ten customers'], correct: 0, explain: 'Data mining uses statistical and computational techniques to uncover patterns, correlations and trends hidden within large volumes of data — for example, identifying which products are commonly bought together — to support decisions such as marketing targeting or inventory planning.', misconception: 'Data mining is sometimes confused with simple data storage or basic reporting, when its distinguishing feature is actively searching for non-obvious patterns and relationships within the data.', tag: 'Data mining (HL)' }
  ];

